## 7. Sign-Up — How It Works

### 7.1 The client side (`src/routes/register.tsx`)

```ts
const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signUp({
  email,
  password,
  options: { data: { name } },
});
if (error) { toast.error(error.message); return; }

const userId = data.user?.id;
if (userId && data.session && selected.length) {
  await supabase.from('enrollments')
    .insert(selected.map(subject_id => ({ user_id: userId, subject_id })));
} else if (selected.length) {
  localStorage.setItem('sf_pending_subjects', JSON.stringify(selected));
}

if (data.session) await supabase.rpc('claim_primary_admin');
toast.success(data.session ? 'Account created' : 'Check your email to confirm your account');
nav({ to: data.session ? '/onboarding' : '/login' });
```

Password minimum is 8 characters; chosen subjects are stored locally when e-mail confirmation is still pending, then applied after the first sign-in.

### 7.2 The database trigger

The moment a row lands in `auth.users`, the profile and default role are created automatically:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user()
RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql SECURITY DEFINER SET search_path = public
AS $$
BEGIN
  INSERT INTO public.profiles(id, name, email)
  VALUES (
    NEW.id,
    COALESCE(NEW.raw_user_meta_data->>'name', split_part(NEW.email,'@',1)),
    COALESCE(NEW.email,'')
  );
  INSERT INTO public.user_roles(user_id, role) VALUES (NEW.id, 'student');
  RETURN NEW;
END $$;

CREATE TRIGGER on_auth_user_created
AFTER INSERT ON auth.users
FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION public.handle_new_user();
```

Every new account is therefore a **student** by default — no one can self-assign a higher role.

